00:33:34.345 --> 00:33:38.016 line:0%
Now people are a lot more
apt to just throw stuff away

00:33:38.116 --> 00:33:41.085 line:0%
and say "Oh that's junk"
and send it to the landfill.

00:33:41.185 --> 00:33:45.356 line:0%
But we understand
the value of junk.

00:33:47.291 --> 00:33:49.560 line:0%
I'm drilling holes into the
pig's teeth and stringing

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:52.930 line:0%
them on to the copper wire.

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:55.099 line:0%
I don't know if most dudes
would do this for their lady

00:33:55.199 --> 00:33:57.435 line:0%
because they'd probably go
out and buy some stupid ring

00:33:57.535 --> 00:34:00.238 line:0%
at the store you know?

00:34:00.338 --> 00:34:04.909 line:0%
Is this starting to
look like something?

00:34:05.009 --> 00:34:07.511 line:0%
I think that Amelia
will like the way that the

00:34:07.611 --> 00:34:10.881 line:0%
necklace looks but I think
even more so Amelia's gonna

00:34:10.981 --> 00:34:13.918 line:0%
like the fact that I was
fidgeting with a rotten

00:34:14.018 --> 00:34:16.520 line:0%
pig's head for her
'cause that's a pretty

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:19.990 line:0%
big deal for me.

00:34:20.090 --> 00:34:22.226 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: As Tony
 puts the finishing touches</i>

00:34:22.326 --> 00:34:24.027 line:0%
<i> on his gift...</i>

00:34:24.127 --> 00:34:26.764 line:0%
<i> back in the swamps of
 south Georgia Colbert's two</i>

00:34:26.864 --> 00:34:29.232 line:0%
<i> daughters are visiting
 to see the progress their</i>

00:34:29.333 --> 00:34:31.102 line:0%
<i> children have made
 with their grandfather.</i>

00:34:31.202 --> 00:34:33.538 line:0%
COLBERT: We've already
erected the first pole for the

00:34:33.638 --> 00:34:36.474 line:0%
corner post for the
cabin and all the kids helped.

00:34:37.140 --> 00:34:38.876 line:0%
Pretty neat huh?

00:34:38.976 --> 00:34:42.112 line:0%
DAUGHTER: He did it once and
he'll be able to do it again

00:34:42.212 --> 00:34:44.648 line:0%
I know he will it'll
just take him some time.

00:34:44.748 --> 00:34:46.317 line:0%
But he'll get it...

00:34:46.417 --> 00:34:49.587 line:0%
he'll do it and it's gonna
be great when he completes it.

00:34:49.687 --> 00:34:51.054 line:0%
COLBERT: Is anybody hungry?

00:34:51.155 --> 00:34:52.590 line:0%
ALL: Yeah.

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:55.393 line:0%
COLBERT: Alright.

00:34:56.527 --> 00:34:58.662 line:0%
You guys did a great job
catching fish and I'm gonna

00:34:58.763 --> 00:35:01.465 line:0%
show you how to cook 'em
without any equipment.

00:35:01.566 --> 00:35:04.701 line:0%
I want them to see how
important it is and take

00:35:04.801 --> 00:35:07.271 line:0%
responsibility for
taking a life...

00:35:07.371 --> 00:35:10.474 line:0%
to use it wisely
and consume it.

00:35:10.574 --> 00:35:12.309 line:0%
So what I do is
open up the gills...

00:35:12.409 --> 00:35:15.146 line:0%
is their breathing apparatus
put my thumb in there and

00:35:15.246 --> 00:35:19.283 line:0%
I twist and that pops the
skin and twist and pop and

00:35:19.383 --> 00:35:22.753 line:0%
twist their head off.

00:35:22.987 --> 00:35:25.055 line:0%
DAUGHTER: I can't do that.

00:35:25.155 --> 00:35:27.324 line:0%
COLBERT: It's just part of
the process of killing the

00:35:27.424 --> 00:35:28.793 line:0%
fish so you can eat it.

00:35:28.893 --> 00:35:31.194 line:0%
What I think is really
gross and weird is that you

00:35:31.294 --> 00:35:32.997 line:0%
read a package and it says

00:35:33.097 --> 00:35:35.432 line:0%
"mechanically
separated chicken."

00:35:35.532 --> 00:35:36.634 line:0%
What does that mean?

00:35:36.734 --> 00:35:39.270 line:0%
Does that mean someone ran
over it with a lawn mower?

00:35:39.370 --> 00:35:42.540 line:0%
You take your thumb and pop
their intestines out and rip

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:45.877 line:0%
like this and rip 'em
right down to their vent.

00:35:45.977 --> 00:35:48.512 line:0%
It's incredibly awesome
and exciting to watch your

00:35:48.613 --> 00:35:51.248 line:0%
grandkids from little
bitty kids teach them

00:35:51.348 --> 00:35:52.616 line:0%
all kinds of stuff...

00:35:52.716 --> 00:35:54.485 line:0%
everything...watch them
grow up and get bigger

00:35:54.585 --> 00:35:55.786 line:0%
and bigger and bigger.

00:35:55.886 --> 00:35:57.722 line:0%
Rip it apart...Rip.

00:35:57.822 --> 00:36:01.359 line:0%
CODY: In the face...ugh!

00:36:05.095 --> 00:36:07.131 line:0%
COLBERT: So what we
really want to accomplish.

00:36:07.231 --> 00:36:09.633 line:0%
Let me check this one.

00:36:09.733 --> 00:36:12.336 line:0%
Yep it's so soft
it's coming apart.

00:36:12.436 --> 00:36:15.038 line:0%
Well what I always say is
you need food and water and

00:36:15.138 --> 00:36:18.142 line:0%
shelter and family.

00:36:18.242 --> 00:36:20.344 line:0%
Mm...now that's fresh fish!

00:36:20.444 --> 00:36:22.145 line:0%
CHELSEA: It's
actually really good.

00:36:22.245 --> 00:36:24.748 line:0%
COLBERT: I've got food and
water and as a family we're

00:36:24.848 --> 00:36:26.617 line:0%
rebuilding the shelter.

00:36:26.717 --> 00:36:27.751 line:0%
What are we doing?

00:36:27.852 --> 00:36:30.120 line:0%
DAUGHTER: You're gonna
stand in between them.

00:36:30.221 --> 00:36:33.424 line:0%
COLBERT: That's a pretty
heartwarming experience.

00:36:33.524 --> 00:36:35.459 line:0%
DAUGHTER 2:
Ah...that's a good face Dad.

00:36:35.559 --> 00:36:38.161 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: While Colbert's
 family heads back to town</i>

00:36:38.261 --> 00:36:40.698 line:0%
<i> back in the Blue Ridge
 Mountains primitive woodsman</i>

00:36:40.798 --> 00:36:43.233 line:0%
<i> Thorn is on a goat-packing
 trip to get medicinal</i>

00:36:43.333 --> 00:36:45.936 line:0%
<i> mushrooms for
 his sick daughter.</i>

00:36:46.037 --> 00:36:49.774 line:0%
THORN: I'm not finding
any reishi...nothing.

00:36:53.744 --> 00:36:57.014 line:0%
I think I see a
squirrel right here.

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:15.433 line:0%
Fresh squirrel is awesome.

00:37:15.533 --> 00:37:17.467 line:0%
You can get 'em off the road
but it's nice to get one

00:37:17.567 --> 00:37:19.403 line:0%
with the bow.

00:37:19.503 --> 00:37:21.538 line:0%
That's one in the bag.

00:37:21.638 --> 00:37:23.507 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: While squirrel
 will make a nice gift</i>

00:37:23.607 --> 00:37:25.008 line:0%
<i> for Tony and Amelia...</i>

00:37:25.108 --> 00:37:27.511 line:0%
<i> Thorn has yet to find
 what he needs most...</i>

00:37:27.611 --> 00:37:29.446 line:0%
<i> medicine for his daughter.</i>

00:37:29.547 --> 00:37:31.782 line:0%
THORN: My little girl
Briar is just so sick.

00:37:31.882 --> 00:37:33.984 line:0%
She's got this
lingering illness.

00:37:34.084 --> 00:37:38.422 line:0%
I've got to get some kind
of strong medicine for her.

00:37:38.522 --> 00:37:42.159 line:0%
It looks like a
pretty good sized one.

00:37:42.893 --> 00:37:44.594 line:0%
It's too old.

00:37:44.695 --> 00:37:47.464 line:0%
Not exactly what
I'm looking for.

00:37:47.564 --> 00:37:49.600 line:0%
I see one down a
little further.

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:53.504 line:0%
Just gotta get to it
without wiping out.

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:58.175 line:0%
Whoa...it's very slick.

00:37:58.275 --> 00:38:01.245 line:0%
Wow this one looks good.

00:38:03.413 --> 00:38:04.615 line:0%
I got it.

00:38:04.715 --> 00:38:07.051 line:0%
I found some
reishi and uh...

00:38:07.151 --> 00:38:09.219 line:0%
take this home and
make some tea for Briar.

00:38:09.319 --> 00:38:12.723 line:0%
Get her on her way
to feeling better.

00:38:12.823 --> 00:38:16.260 line:0%
C'mon...Good job.

00:38:29.573 --> 00:38:31.107 line:0%
THORN: Hey y'all.

00:38:31.207 --> 00:38:33.310 line:0%
AMELIA: Hey...you're back!

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:34.545 line:0%
THORN: Made it.

00:38:34.645 --> 00:38:36.113 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: In
 North Carolina...</i>

00:38:36.213 --> 00:38:38.415 line:0%
<i> Thorn has returned from a
 long hike into the mountains</i>

00:38:38.515 --> 00:38:41.085 line:0%
<i> with a gift for
 Tony and Amelia.</i>

00:38:41.185 --> 00:38:42.486 line:0%
AMELIA: You got a squirrel!

00:38:42.586 --> 00:38:44.221 line:0%
THORN: Got a squirrel...
some meat.

00:38:44.321 --> 00:38:45.589 line:0%
AMELIA: Nice.

00:38:45.689 --> 00:38:46.924 line:0%
THORN: Got some
medicine for Briar.

00:38:47.024 --> 00:38:48.758 line:0%
Did you have fun
with Tony and Amelia?

00:38:48.859 --> 00:38:50.594 line:0%
BRIAR: I want to stay
here for a little while.

00:38:50.694 --> 00:38:52.763 line:0%
THORN: You want to stay
here a little bit longer?

00:38:52.863 --> 00:38:54.698 line:0%
AMELIA: We'll
borrow you any time.

00:38:54.798 --> 00:38:56.533 line:0%
THORN: Well... y'all are
welcome to that squirrel.

00:38:56.633 --> 00:38:57.868 line:0%
AMELIA: Thank you!

00:38:57.968 --> 00:38:59.002 line:0%
THORN: Thanks so much.

00:38:59.103 --> 00:39:01.772 line:0%
We'll see you
around for sure.

00:39:02.072 --> 00:39:04.608 line:0%
Bye.

00:39:05.976 --> 00:39:08.045 line:0%
TONY: Amelia and I have
lived here together for

00:39:08.145 --> 00:39:10.747 line:0%
three years now in
the forest garden.

00:39:10.847 --> 00:39:13.550 line:0%
We're gonna celebrate our
anniversary by eating a

00:39:13.650 --> 00:39:16.753 line:0%
bunch of delicious food
from the forest garden.

00:39:17.487 --> 00:39:20.523 line:0%
AMELIA: Gotta include
some meat with our feast.

00:39:20.624 --> 00:39:23.694 line:0%
So squirrel's the
perfect thing to add to our

00:39:23.794 --> 00:39:28.132 line:0%
vegetables and
eggs and berries.

00:39:33.370 --> 00:39:38.175 line:0%
Oh flowers...wow.

00:39:38.275 --> 00:39:39.743 line:0%
TONY: So I made
you something.

00:39:39.843 --> 00:39:41.078 line:0%
AMELIA: You
made me something?

00:39:41.178 --> 00:39:43.647 line:0%
TONY: Mhm...I sure did
and it's not a swale...

00:39:43.747 --> 00:39:45.049 line:0%
it's not a house.

00:39:45.149 --> 00:39:48.251 line:0%
It's an abstract
representation of awesome.

00:39:49.052 --> 00:39:52.957 line:0%
AMELIA: Wow...oh my
gosh it's beautiful!

00:39:53.057 --> 00:39:55.359 line:0%
It's my first piece
of jewelry from you.

00:39:55.459 --> 00:39:58.595 line:0%
TONY: This is the first piece
of jewelry I've ever made.

00:39:58.695 --> 00:40:00.363 line:0%
AMELIA: Was it hard
to pull them out?

00:40:00.463 --> 00:40:02.699 line:0%
TONY: Yeah and it
was pretty gross.

00:40:02.799 --> 00:40:04.434 line:0%
Smelled bad...
smelled like rotten pig.

00:40:04.534 --> 00:40:05.903 line:0%
AMELIA: Oh my God.

00:40:06.003 --> 00:40:07.371 line:0%
TONY: But I went
ahead and did it.

00:40:07.471 --> 00:40:08.672 line:0%
AMELIA: I'm impressed.

00:40:08.772 --> 00:40:10.040 line:0%
I can't believe
you did this.

00:40:10.141 --> 00:40:11.809 line:0%
TONY: Yeah...I can't
believe I made it either.

00:40:11.909 --> 00:40:13.944 line:0%
It's just mildly stinky.

00:40:14.044 --> 00:40:18.315 line:0%
AMELIA: That'll go away.

00:40:18.415 --> 00:40:20.851 line:0%
COLBERT: What I've learned
from the loss of my cabin is

00:40:20.951 --> 00:40:23.587 line:0%
letting go of an intense
emotional attachment to

00:40:23.687 --> 00:40:26.957 line:0%
something I've
made that I built.

00:40:27.291 --> 00:40:29.760 line:0%
And in that sense I
feel wealthy...because

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:31.628 line:0%
all of nature...
if I need it...

00:40:31.728 --> 00:40:36.000 line:0%
is available for my
simple...physical needs.

00:40:37.935 --> 00:40:43.007 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:40:43.841 --> 00:40:45.609 line:0%
<i> [Gabriel makes bird call].</i>

00:40:45.709 --> 00:40:47.444 line:0%
LUNA: Hey...oh my gosh...

00:40:47.544 --> 00:40:48.712 line:0%
you're back!

00:40:48.812 --> 00:40:50.147 line:0%
GABRIEL: Hey!

00:40:50.247 --> 00:40:52.449 line:0%
<i> [greeting each
 other inaudibly].</i>

00:40:52.549 --> 00:40:55.151 line:0%
Not many women would
support somebody to run off

00:40:55.251 --> 00:40:58.922 line:0%
for three months and trust
that I'm 100% there for her

00:40:59.022 --> 00:41:01.558 line:0%
and that's the reason that
I'm ready to marry her and

00:41:01.658 --> 00:41:03.293 line:0%
take it to the next step.

00:41:03.393 --> 00:41:05.262 line:0%
I paddled out and
fished a little bit.

00:41:05.362 --> 00:41:07.130 line:0%
I actually brought one back.

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:09.667 line:0%
LUNA: Mmm...awesome.

00:41:09.767 --> 00:41:13.436 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:41:13.537 --> 00:41:15.605 line:0%
THORN: This'll
help your cough.

00:41:15.705 --> 00:41:17.274 line:0%
Just drop 'em right in.

00:41:17.374 --> 00:41:18.775 line:0%
They won't hurt you.

00:41:18.875 --> 00:41:21.711 line:0%
Life's looking pretty good
out here and I think heading

00:41:21.812 --> 00:41:25.048 line:0%
into this next fall
and this next winter...

00:41:25.148 --> 00:41:26.282 line:0%
Here you go.

00:41:26.383 --> 00:41:29.386 line:0%
I've got a lot of
knowledge that I've acquired

00:41:29.486 --> 00:41:32.022 line:0%
from just trying.

00:41:32.122 --> 00:41:37.094 line:0%
It's a good feeling to
feel at home in the woods.

00:41:37.194 --> 00:41:40.364 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:41:40.464 --> 00:41:43.233 line:0%
AMELIA: We've gotten
really far and I mean...

00:41:43.333 --> 00:41:45.535 line:0%
there's still a long way to
go but jeez...we have come a

00:41:45.635 --> 00:41:48.138 line:0%
really long way
this past year.

00:41:48.239 --> 00:41:50.707 line:0%
So maybe next year
we'll be having our spring

00:41:50.807 --> 00:41:55.545 line:0%
anniversary with a third
little human here with us.

00:41:56.246 --> 00:41:57.882 line:0%
TONY: The only babies I
want around here are ones

00:41:57.982 --> 00:42:00.951 line:0%
that I can eat.

00:42:02.552 --> 00:42:06.090 line:0%
AMELIA: Happy three-year
forest garden anniversary.

00:42:09.159 --> 00:42:10.760 line:0%
LUNA: Cheers.

00:42:10.860 --> 00:42:14.131 line:0%
GABRIEL: Yeah!

00:42:16.233 --> 00:42:19.235 line:0%
COLBERT: This is
my lookout spot.

00:42:19.335 --> 00:42:21.205 line:0%
I used to come up
here when I was younger.

00:42:21.305 --> 00:42:24.441 line:0%
Now I need a
ladder to get up here.

00:42:24.741 --> 00:42:27.778 line:0%
I come out and climb
it once in a while.

00:42:27.878 --> 00:42:29.946 line:0%
A lot of people ask me
how old I am and I say well

00:42:30.046 --> 00:42:31.848 line:0%
I'm just a teenager.

00:42:31.948 --> 00:42:33.650 line:0%
I'm kind of an
antique teenager but I'm

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:35.619 line:0%
just a teenager.

00:42:35.719 --> 00:42:37.921 line:0%
I've been a
teenager for fifty years.

00:42:38.021 --> 00:42:40.558 line:0%
<i> [laughs].</i>

00:42:42.092 --> 00:42:48.565 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:42:48.666 --> 00:42:51.101 line:0%
GABRIEL: This has been
an awesome experience.

00:42:51.201 --> 00:42:53.336 line:0%
I'm really grateful for
the time out on the land.

00:42:53.436 --> 00:42:55.672 line:0%
I put my whole soul and
energy into it and I've

00:42:55.772 --> 00:42:59.076 line:0%
gotten a lot out of it.

00:42:59.176 --> 00:43:03.714 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:43:07.117 --> 00:43:09.786 line:0%
THORN: There has been
some tough times out here.

00:43:09.886 --> 00:43:11.455 line:0%
But that's okay.

00:43:11.555 --> 00:43:14.758 line:0%
There's
struggles everywhere.

00:43:18.261 --> 00:43:21.298 line:0%
TONY: Amelia and I were
born into a culture of making

00:43:21.398 --> 00:43:24.034 line:0%
money and then taking it
to buy your things and we

00:43:24.134 --> 00:43:26.870 line:0%
dropped out of that culture.

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:29.239 line:0%
So now we're trying to pull
together fragments that make

00:43:29.339 --> 00:43:33.176 line:0%
sense and glue them together
and make what I think is a

00:43:33.276 --> 00:43:36.013 line:0%
better life for ourselves.

00:43:40.084 --> 00:43:42.653 line:0%
COLBERT: Everything in
nature has found a niche...

00:43:42.753 --> 00:43:45.222 line:0%
a perfect harmony...
a balance.

00:43:45.322 --> 00:43:49.760 line:0%
Man needs to find his niche
his perfect balance where he

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:52.096 line:0%
fits in the natural cycles.

00:43:52.196 --> 00:43:55.766 line:0%
You gotta find yourself.

00:44:01.438 --> 00:44:03.806 line:0%
I feel like a little kid
in the Garden of Eden.

00:44:03.906 --> 00:44:06.944 line:0%
Just playing in the woods.

